# Glossary

This file is the map. Every other file in the Sales workbook is reachable from
here, and reading it teaches the one thing the whole format leans on: **how a
workbook refers to itself**. Nothing here is decorative. The links resolve, the
references type-check, and the build follows the same edges you read below.

A workbook is not a pile of files; it is a **code graph**. Each unit is a node,
and the way you mention another unit — by name, by `:atom`, by `[[backlink]]`,
by `work://` link — becomes a typed edge in that graph. The compiler walks
these edges at the weave gate to resolve dependencies, audit capabilities, and
decide what ships. So addressing is not paperwork. Addressing IS the program's
structure.

## The five ways to address something

There are exactly five reference forms in the format, and each one means a
different kind of edge. The function below is real: it is the resolver the
weave gate uses to classify a raw reference token before it becomes a graph
edge. Read it as the spec for everything that follows.

defmodule Glossary do
  @moduledoc """
  Resolves a raw reference token to a typed code-graph edge.
  The build feeds every token the three extractors find (prose, AST,
  generated WIT) through here; the edge kind drives audit + bundling.
  """

  defstruct from: nil, to: nil, kind: nil, queryable: false

  @kinds [:unit_ref, :type_ref, :backlink, :tag, :deep_link]

  def classify(token, origin) do
    cond do
      String.starts_with?(token, "work://") ->
        edge(origin, strip_scheme(token), :deep_link)

      String.starts_with?(token, "[[") ->
        edge(origin, resolve_backlink(token), :backlink)

      String.starts_with?(token, "#") ->
        %__MODULE__{from: origin, to: String.trim_leading(token, "#"),
                    kind: :tag, queryable: true}

      String.starts_with?(token, ":") ->
        edge(origin, String.trim_leading(token, ":"), :unit_ref)

      String.match?(token, ~r/^[A-Z]\w+$/) ->
        edge(origin, token, :type_ref)

      true ->
        {:error, {:unresolved, token}}
    end
  end

  defp edge(from, to, kind) when kind in @kinds do
    %__MODULE__{from: from, to: to, kind: kind}
  end

  # A backlink resolves leniently. We normalize the label — case-fold it, drop a
  # leading "the", drop a trailing " agent"/" kit" — and match the result against
  # unit names AND file titles alike. So [[score]], [[Score]] and [[Scoring a
  # lead]] all reach the same node: authors write the natural word, not the exact
  # title. Only a label that normalizes to nothing in the symbol table is dangling.
  defp resolve_backlink(token), do: token |> unwrap_backlink() |> normalize()

  defp unwrap_backlink(token), do: token |> String.trim_leading("[[") |> String.trim_trailing("]]")

  defp normalize(label) do
    label
    |> String.downcase()
    |> String.replace_leading("the ", "")
    |> String.replace_trailing(" agent", "")
    |> String.replace_trailing(" kit", "")
    |> String.trim()
  end

  defp strip_scheme(token), do: String.replace_prefix(token, "work://", "")
end

Walking the five forms, with the meaning each carries:

- **`:atom` unit reference.** Writing `:score` in prose links to the unit named
  `score`. It becomes an `:unit_ref` edge and, if that unit is a dependency,
  the audit demands the corresponding grant. This is the workhorse — most
  prose mentions of code are `:atom` references.
- **`@type` / capitalized type reference.** Writing `Lead` or `@statuses` links
  to a declared structure. It becomes a `:type_ref` edge, so the dataflow
  checker knows a value of that shape crosses the boundary.
- **`[[backlink]]`.** A `[[Backlink]]` resolves to a node **by name, leniently**:
  the label is normalized (case-folded, a leading *the* and a trailing *agent*/
  *kit* dropped) and matched against unit names and file titles alike. So
  `[[score]]`, `[[Score]]`, and `[[Scoring a lead]]` all reach the same unit —
  write the natural word, not the exact title. It is bidirectional in the graph:
  the target knows who points at it. This file links to [[Pipeline]] below.
- **`#tag`.** A `#tag` is the only **queryable** form — note `queryable: true`
  in `classify/2`. Tags are not labels you sprinkle for looks; they are an
  index you can run `work near #b2b` against. We use exactly one real tag in
  this workbook, `#b2b`, and it earns its place by being queried.
- **`work://` deep link.** A `work://` URI addresses a unit (or a region inside
  one) from anywhere — another workbook, a chat message, a task `ref`. It is the
  stable external address: `work://sales/score` survives a file move because it
  names the unit, not the path.

## The canonical structures

Every file in the workbook speaks in the same two structures. They are declared
once, here, and referenced by `@type` everywhere else. A record is just a
`defstruct` — field types are **inferred from the default values**, never
written as a typespec. An enum is a set of atoms. A variant is a tagged tuple.

defmodule Lead do
  @moduledoc "The unit of work. Flows :new -> :enriched -> :scored -> :contacted."
  defstruct name: "", domain: "", revenue: 0, employees: 0, score: 0, status: :new
end

@statuses [:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]

defmodule Task do
  @moduledoc "A tracked piece of work; owner resolves to a person, ref is a work:// link."
  defstruct title: "", status: :todo, owner: nil, ref: nil
end

The `Lead.status` field moves through `@statuses` in order, and a `Task.status`
is one of `:todo | :doing | :done` — a variant expressed as an atom set, not a
DSL. When `:enrich` returns `{:ok, %Lead{}}` or `{:error, reason}`, that tagged
tuple IS the type; there is nothing else to declare.

## The unit index

Each entry below names a unit, says where it runs, and links its home file. The
**placement** column is load-bearing: there are only two places code runs,
`client` (the browser, as wasm) and `server` (the nexus, native BEAM). A
**sandbox** is *not* a placement — it is a named isolation unit a server unit
nests inside. A unit with no body has no placement at all.

Compute and flow, all server-side:

- `:score` — server compute. `score(%Lead{}) -> integer`, revenue points plus
  size points, rounded. Pure, no grants. See [[Score]].
- `:enrich` — server compute, `grant: [net: "api.crm.com"]`. Fills a `Lead`'s
  `domain`, `revenue`, `employees` and sets `status: :enriched`. See [[Enrich]].
- `:pipeline` — a flow, not a function: seed leads, `:enrich`, `:score`, rank.
  Orchestrates the others. See [[Pipeline]].
- `:tax` — server compute nested in `sandbox :pricing`. See [[Pricing under a sandbox]].
- `:fx` — server compute in `sandbox :rates` (`grant: [:net]`), exchange rates,
  called cross-sandbox from `:tax`. Caps flow by grant, never inheritance.
- `:forecast` — sandbox compute, Rust orchestrating a Zig numeric kernel; both
  compile to wasm and interoperate via the generated component contract. See
  [[Forecast]].
- `:risk` — Python sandbox compute, a risk score from a `Lead`'s features.

The shared interface and the client islands:

- `FinModel` — a **kit**: a module the team imports for `ltv/1`, `cac/1`,
  `npv/2`. A kit is just an `@behaviour` plus its implementation, packaged.
  Home: `kits/finmodel.work`, addressable as `work://sales/kits/finmodel`.
- `:chart` — client Svelte island, a bar chart of lead scores. Hydrates in the
  browser. See [[Chart]].
- `:sparkline` — client SolidJS island, a score-trend sparkline.

The agents (runtime-backed brains; loops run in server Elixir, memory is typed
data):

- `analyst` — drafts outreach from a scored `Lead`.
- `coder` — a ReAct agent with sandboxed bash tools.
- `researcher` — an auto-researcher loop: plan, search, verify, synthesize.
- `reflexion` — generate, critique, revise, with the trace as its memory.

## How the graph gets queried

Because references are typed edges, the same three CLI verbs work at build time
and at runtime. The snippet below is the real query layer the `work near` verb
calls — it walks `Glossary` edges, so a `#b2b` tag and a `:score` mention are
both first-class lookups.

defmodule Glossary.Query do
  @moduledoc "Backs `work check` / `work why` / `work near` over the code graph."

  def near(edges, target) do
    edges
    |> Enum.filter(fn e -> e.to == target or e.from == target end)
    |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.kind)
  end

  def why(edges, from, to) do
    edges
    |> Enum.filter(fn e -> e.from == from and e.to == to end)
    |> case do
      [] -> {:error, :no_edge}
      hits -> {:ok, Enum.map(hits, & &1.kind)}
    end
  end

  def tagged(edges, tag) do
    Enum.filter(edges, fn e -> e.kind == :tag and e.to == tag end)
  end
end

So `work why :pipeline :enrich` returns `{:ok, [:unit_ref]}` because this file
and [[Pipeline]] both mention `:enrich`, and `work near #b2b` returns every node
that carries the one queryable tag we chose to maintain. The links you read are
the graph the tools query — read the rest of the workbook by following them.
